memo_weighing.ops:
Pope's seat runs on Boeing's operations doctrine; she has no airplane-business evidence (Very low), so I weighed her memo as doctrine-backed advice. Her KPI veto does not bind: the brief reports no live problem.

Her results gave the Board what it asked for:
- one clean-sheet development is within engineering capacity, with zero strain on the fps alone;
- the supply base is ready, on the condition that we fund fragile suppliers (engines first) and keep them hot;
- Solo over via Embraer on IP: Embraer brings no capital or capacity we lack;
- code 7 is manageable on the 10-year, with GTF2 and UltraFan NB maturing 4 and 3 years before EIS and CFM certifying first.

Her on-time delivery test carried weight with me. The 10-year's 2040 date is the 7-year's central slip case, so it carries headroom without concurrency [BX-1263, BX-1275]. The 7-year has zero headroom, and UltraFan NB would be ready only in its EIS year. Her 'fail' on the 7-year ramp is advice, not a veto, and I treat it so.

She flags the 2040 engine-source count as at risk on a 2040 EIS. The GM's scoring decides that, and I note it.

board_response.response:
Taken up in full; see rationale §1.

H1 is lifted for 2030 on all of the cited evidence:
- 777X first delivery 2027 [BX-0550] and cash-positive from 2029 [BX-0547];
- MAX 7/10 certification expected in 2026 [BX-1873], treated as done since the brief reports no trouble;
- my July 2026 'about two more years' [BG-0068];
- net debt at $0 at end-2029 (base) and end-2030 (stress);
- the three work streams converged [B-2303, BX-1296].

The lift was conditional on Malave's buffer and fundability tests and Pope's readiness results, and all passed.

H3's first half no longer binds as a hard rule, because its 777X-first ground [B-2276] has closed. I still hold the Re-engine, under H3's second half, H4, your -$1B limit and one-development-at-a-time.

Taken up. The brief's cell for fps 10-yr + 787 hold is 2029 / yes / yes ($0.0B) / 1 (3) in every column.

Malave's reconstruction:
- base net debt $16.5B (2027), $6.7B (2028), $0 every year from 2029;
- stress $18.3B, $11.7B, $3.3B (2029), then $0 from 2030, with at least $2.5B of headroom in the 2036 peak year;
- programme cash about $3.0B in 2030, peaking around $8.4B in 2036.

I recommend no shareholder returns and no new equity until we are solidly investment grade. Those decisions stay with the Board.

Taken up on all three points:
- the public statement gives a 2040 EIS with headroom built in, and no other dates;
- the premium ($0.46B) is logged with its reason;
- the launch goes to the Aerospace Safety Committee with the Chief Engineer's readiness view before the full Board votes (a private other move). After that, KPI-based stage gates and, if needed, one conservative reset.

frame.asks_cfo:
Jay, you are my independent check on the estimates [BX-1316]. Run your buffer test as the baseline case, not as a sensitivity [BX-0553].

1. **Slip leg.** Confirm each row's S5 result against the -5.64 floor:
   - 10-year -0.78; 7-year -0.83;
   - 10-year plus Re-engine +1.42;
   - 10-year plus rate -1.52; 7-year plus rate -1.57;
   - via Embraer -18.83, which fails by $13.19B.
   Name every row you veto.
2. **Fundable when the 777X turns.** The 777X is cash-positive from 2029 [BX-0547]. Confirm that a 2030 launch passes.
3. **Debt penalty.** Confirm the grid already nets it: undiscounted, 10-year $19.1B, 7-year $22.0B, via Embraer $52.5B, Re-engine $0.6B. Quote these for scale only; never subtract them a second time.
4. **Cash timing on the tracker's curve.** The bills:
   - 10-year: $60.32B over 2030-40;
   - 7-year: $65.00B over 2030-37;
   - Re-engine: $5B over 2030-35, plus $1.2B of strain beside the 10-year;
   - Rate Increase: $2.94B by 2032.
   For plans B and C, give me the base and stress net-debt paths. Tell me which year the 7-year's stress peak of $0.7B lands in, and whether it breaches the 2035 hold year. Confirm there is no new equity and no shareholder return in either plan.
5. **7-year or 10-year.** The 10-year trails by 0.46 in S2 and S4 and leads by 1.30 in S1 and S3. Which is the higher-confidence plan "from a schedule and cost perspective"?
6. **The 787 Re-engine beside the 10-year.** It adds +2.20 in S1, S2 and S5 and -3.58 in S3 and S4. Does it pass your buffer? Is it worth the second development and the debt? I take this as advice; the Board's limit is $1B.
7. **Rate Increase.** Confirm that it loses 0.74 in every column beside any fps, so it fails its business case.
8. **Airbus.** Give me your expected column, and confirm that S5 is the right slip leg this round.

