recommendations.text:
The CFO flags that, on the FY2025 base, the dividend would draw about $2.45B a year from net cash, which would exhaust $13.4B within the development years. Before the 2035 round the Board asks for:
- a net-cash bridge for 2030-2036 that includes the dividend, on both the FY2025 base and the 2030 run-rate;
- confirmation of the payout guardrail (lower end of the 30-50% range if ramp cash does not arrive, no equity raise, no new return policy);
- the effect of the single $35.52B charge at the 2037 EIS on reported earnings and on the payout base.
Refundable advances, state support and risk-sharing are upside and are never assumed in the case.

board_response.response:
Taken up. The statement:
- names no rival engine maker and blames no one;
- has no price or terms;
- gives no Ducted, Embraer or GEnx date and no Open Fan entry year at all ('it's really not for us to say' [CX-0205]);
- makes no fuel-burn or durability claim for the ducted engine and never calls it next-generation;
- keeps RISE as our next generation.

frame.situation:
PUBLIC RECORD, 2030. No programme has launched; everyone flies today's products (737 MAX, 787, A320neo, A350; LEAP, GTF, GEnx, Trent). Shares were flat over 2026-29:
- NB airframes: Boeing 40%, Airbus 60%.
- NB engines: CFM 76%, P&W 24%, RR 0%. LEAP is sole source on the 737 and shares the A320neo with the GTF.
- WB engines: GEnx 42%, Trent 58%.
No rival new NB engine is launched, committed or selected, and no Joint Venture commitment is on the record.

STILL OPEN.
- fps: the 7-year version enters service in 2037; the 10-year version and the Embraer route in 2040.
- NGSA: enters service in 2037.
- Both widebody Re-engines: enter service 5 years after launch.

OUR POSITION.
- ΔPV is +0.00 if nobody moves. EV $160B, debt $35B.
- Objective, NB at least 50% in 2040, 2045 and 2050: met today at 76%. It is at risk only in the columns where a rival takes our slot (S3, S6).
- Objective, Open Fan launched and ready by 2050: not met. Only a launch by the 2035 round can still meet it, since launch + 10 must be 2050 or earlier.

GRID READING.
- Hold loses -32.07 (S2) to -105.01 (S6) in every column where an airframer launches, because a new airframe splits our sole-source 737 slot and our A320neo slot.
- S3 (NGSA, code 2) and S6 (codes 1, 2) are the columns where a rival selection takes CFM's slot. That is the hard-rule-3 trigger on the grid, since no rival engine is on the record.
- Best plan by column: GEnx alone in S1 (+0.78) and S6 (-104.23); Ducted + Partner Embraer + GEnx in S2 to S5 (-20.69, -71.86, -50.10, -48.96).
- Every Open Fan row loses -8.00 to -57.60 against hold.

BOARD GUIDANCE (non-binding, labelled as such). The Board would VETO:
- an Open Fan this round in any combination: it waits in every launch column, and a 2030 launch is ready in 2045, the same year as a 2035 launch;
- Lobby without a live Open Fan;
- GEnx beside a single other project (-1.22, strain);
- Partner Embraer with no new CFM engine, or with S1 as the expected column;
- a Ducted if any of these holds: (a) S1 or S6 is expected and no slot-taking column is named at a weighted value of zero or better; (b) the premium is over $2.0B; (c) my 20% line, or an Ali durability or date flag, stands unanswered; (d) its value rests on a ready year before 2036.
It would APPROVE a defensive Ducted, alone or as Ducted + Embraer + GEnx, when the expected column is S2 to S5 or my weights carry it. In return it wants:
- weights for all six columns;
- Ali's ready years against the first new NB entry;
- the Safran gate for each order;
- the 70% payout kept whole;
- new R&D plus strain of about $1.0B a year;
- the RISE allowance unspent;
- a public statement with no rival named, no blame, no Open Fan entry before 2045, no untested Ducted date and no price talk.

tests.numbers:
The package's worst column is S6 at -105.61, -84.92 below S2 (-20.69). Its worst item column is S1, at -6.00 against GEnx alone: the bill, no more. A 2035 cancel writes off $3.33B of the Ducted (saving $0.67B); with the strain sunk (inference), S1 comes to about -5.33. Ask (5): yes, S1's -4.00 (or -6.00 for the package) is the most it can lose. If an airframer launches in 2035 (entry 2042), a 2030 Ducted ready in 2036 earns about +$8.9B gross, or +$4.9B net (inference, calibrated to the grid's S2 value). A GEnx or Embraer deferred to 2035 would, on my reading, draw the $2B strain while the Ducted is in development to 2036 (inference), and Embraer would also lose 2037-41. The grid leaves out:
- moves after this round: a rival GTF2, UltraFan or Joint Venture launch, a code change before entry, airframer launches in 2035;
- a slip from the 2036 ready year: one year of float to 2037, then about $1.0-1.2B a year in S2. Our own supplier-dependent dates slipped before;
- the supply load of three projects (Ali's call);
- a widebody Re-engine on a rival engine;
- a slowdown in airframer schedules, tariffs or a recession.
Objective (ask 6): NB at least 50% is met at 76% in S1 for every non-Open-Fan row. It is at risk in S3 and S6 for every row: in S3 we keep only the Boeing slot, so share is about 43% in 2040 even with Ducted and Embraer (my arithmetic of the rules, inference). On the same arithmetic, the code-7 columns S4 and S5 fall to about 43-44% in 2040 without the Ducted and come back above 50% with it. Check this against the GM's share line.

